"type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "expanded", "type": "name"}, {"content": "enlarged, widened ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "expanded", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We define nonterminal symbols using grammar rules that show us the expanded sequence of detailed tokens.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob08aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "sequence", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a series of related facts or things", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We can use the Extended Backus Form to define grammar. This notation differentiates terminal symbols, single tokens, from nonterminal symbols, sequence of tokens.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob08aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "detailed", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a complete description of something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: This notation differentiates terminal symbols or single tokens, from nonterminal symbols, that means a sequence of tokens.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob08aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "fragment", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a piece or part of whole", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  The EBNF defines context-free grammars that always generate the same token fragments in the nonterminal variables.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob08aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Search more information about how to read grammar in language syntax. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Design a concept map to explain what grammar in language syntax is and its features. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Use the grammar covered in this unit to write your ideas. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Show your concept map to your classmates and discuss your ideas with them.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I look for more information on grammar in language syntax.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I create my own concept map to explain what grammar in language syntax is. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share my ideas about grammar in language syntax with my classmates. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Verbs with Infinitive", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "There are some common verbs in English that introduce another action with the preposition <strong>to</strong> and the infinitive form of the verb.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When you introduce an action with verbs such as, want, would like, and other verbs to talk about likes and dislikes; you have to use the <strong>infinitive </strong>form of the verb preceded by the preposition <strong>to</strong>. </br></br>     For example: </br> We would like <strong>to buy</strong> a new laptop. </br> I want <strong>to study </strong>computer systems engineering. </br></br> The difference between <strong>like to</strong> and <strong>would like to</strong> is that <strong>Would like</strong> is used when we want to ask for or request something in a polite way.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Verbs followed by the infinitives\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Like to&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Want to</br> Hate to &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Love to</br> Decide to &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Attempt to </br> Have to\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "I would like to practice the topic of language syntax.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I want to add that in order to avoid ambiguity in our sentences, we have to add as many conditions as necessary. ", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Modal Can for Possibility", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use <strong>can</strong> to talk about possibility or ability", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use the <strong>modal verb can</strong> when you want to express possibility. A modal verb has special characteristics: </br> </br>  1.\tIt has no conjugation, it's the same for all subject pronouns. </br> 2.\tA modal verb is never introduced by a preposition, they usually come after or before subjects. </br> 3.\tModal verbs don't need auxiliary verbs to make interrogative or negative statements. </br> </br>  Examples: </br> I <strong>can </strong>read the grammar of the program. </br> Tom <strong>can</strong> read the grammar too. </br> We <strong>cannot</strong> understand the topic. </br> <strong>Can </strong>you make statements using tokens? </br> </br> Modal verbs act as their own auxiliaries when creating negative or interrogative statements.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "We cannot fail that exam. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The syntax of a language defines whether or not the language is legal and we can execute it successfully. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We can use the EBNF to verify if a statement is legal, right?", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can give information to understand how to read grammars in language syntax. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can explain different features related to language syntax and its grammars. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can identify detailed information about grammars in language syntax.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "How can we verify the legality of a program? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"co